course: everyday
lesson: 38
title: Simple cooking and a healthy breakfast
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: After a night's sleep your body has gone many hours without food, so breakfast gives it fuel for the morning's thinking and playing.
explain: A healthy breakfast mixes different kinds of food: something with whole grains for steady energy (oats, wholemeal toast), something with protein to keep you full (eggs, milk, yoghurt, beans, nuts) and some fruit.
explain: A breakfast that is mostly sugar, like many sweet cereals or pastries, gives a quick burst of energy and then leaves you hungry and tired before lunch.
explain: For example, porridge made with milk and topped with a sliced banana is a simple, filling breakfast that a child can help make with an adult.
explain: Simple cooking still has rules: wash your hands, wash fruit, use the microwave and toaster safely, and open hot containers away from your face because steam burns.
explain: A family in Ohio ate chicken that was still pink in the middle at a barbecue; several of them got food poisoning and were sick for days.
explain: A boy in Leeds put a metal spoon in a bowl in the microwave; it sparked and set off a small fire, and the microwave was ruined.
fact: Breakfast gives the body fuel after many hours without food.
fact: Whole grains such as oats and wholemeal bread give energy that lasts.
fact: Protein from eggs, milk, yoghurt, beans or nuts helps you feel full for longer.
fact: Many breakfast cereals contain a lot of added sugar, which you can see on the label.
fact: Water or milk is a better breakfast drink than fizzy drinks.
fact: Fruit should be washed before it is eaten.
fact: Metal, including foil and metal spoons, must never go into a microwave.
fact: You must never poke a knife or fork into a toaster, even when it is switched off at the wall.
fact: Food heated in a microwave can be very hot in some spots and cool in others, so it should be stirred.
fact: Steam from a hot container can burn, so you open the lid away from your face.
fact: Eggs should be cooked until the white is firm.
fact: Raw and undercooked chicken can carry germs that cause food poisoning.
fact: Metal in a microwave can spark and start a fire.
exercise: Why is breakfast important? | It gives your body fuel after many hours without food | It makes you grow taller overnight while you are fast asleep | It stops you needing any lunch | It is only important on school days
exercise: Which breakfast will keep you full longest? | Porridge with milk and a banana | A can of fizzy drink | A big bowl of sugar-coated cereal flakes | A chocolate bar
exercise: Which food is a good source of protein? | Eggs | Jam | Apple juice | White sugar
exercise: Why do sugary breakfasts often leave you hungry before lunch? | They give a quick burst of energy that soon runs out | They have far too much protein in them for the body to use | They take all morning to digest | They make your stomach grow bigger
exercise: Where can you find out how much sugar is in a cereal? | On the nutrition label on the box | On the picture on the front of the box | By looking at its colour | From the size of the box
exercise: Which drink is best with breakfast? | A glass of milk or water | A can of cola | An energy drink | A large milkshake with syrup
exercise: Why must you never put foil in a microwave? | Metal can spark and start a fire | Foil makes the food taste of metal | Foil keeps the food from cooking at all | The microwave only heats foil
exercise: A piece of toast is stuck in the toaster. What is the safe thing to do? | Ask an adult to unplug it and help | Poke it out carefully with a metal fork | Shake the toaster while it is on | Pour water into the toaster
exercise: Why should you stir soup heated in a microwave? | Microwaves heat unevenly, leaving very hot spots | Stirring makes the soup taste of more | The microwave only heats the very top layer of the soup | Soup sinks to the bottom without stirring
exercise: How should you lift the lid of a bowl just out of the microwave? | Away from your face so the steam does not burn you | Toward your face so you can smell whether it is ready | Quickly, right up to your nose | With your bare fingers in the middle
exercise: Why should you wash an apple before eating it? | To remove dirt and germs from its skin | To make it much sweeter and juicier to eat | To make it crunchier to bite | To remove its vitamins
exercise: Which of these is a whole grain? | Oats | White sugar | Butter | Cheese
exercise: When is a fried egg cooked enough to be safe? | When the white is firm, not runny | As soon as it touches the pan | When it starts to smell nice | When the yolk is still cold inside
test: A boy in Melbourne has a big bowl of chocolate cereal and is starving by 10 o'clock. What is the best explanation? | The sugar gave quick energy that did not last | He ate too much protein for breakfast | Cereal makes the stomach work too fast | Chocolate takes much longer to digest than oats do
test: Which breakfast has whole grains, protein and fruit? | Wholemeal toast, an egg and an orange | Two pastries and a fizzy drink | A bowl of jelly and some sweets | A cup of hot chocolate with sugar and cream only
test: What would most likely happen if someone put a metal spoon in the microwave with their soup? | Sparks could fly and start a fire | The spoon would make the soup heat faster | The spoon would keep the soup cool | Nothing, metal is fine in microwaves
test: Which statement is true? | A toaster can still give a shock if you poke metal into it | A toaster is safe to poke once it is cool | Toasters cannot conduct electricity at all, they are plastic | Only the outside of a toaster is dangerous
test: A girl in Halifax takes off the cover of a hot bowl of pasta and her face goes red. What went wrong? | She lifted it toward her face and the steam burned her | The pasta was too cold to eat | The microwave sprayed water on her | The microwave leaked dangerous rays out onto her face
test: Why is plain yoghurt with berries a better choice than a doughnut for breakfast? | It has protein and fruit with less added sugar | It is more sugary so it gives more energy | It has no food value but tastes nice | Doughnuts are made of whole grains
test: Mum's microwave porridge is lukewarm at the edge. Why might it still burn you? | Microwaves heat unevenly and the middle can be very hot | Cold porridge burns more than hot porridge | The bowl makes the porridge hotter and hotter as it cools | Porridge never gets hot in a microwave
test: Which is the best way to start cooking breakfast at home? | Wash your hands and ask an adult to help | Turn on every ring on the stove first | Start with the sharpest knife you can find | Cook alone so nobody gets in the way
test: An egg is cooked but the white is still clear and runny. What should you do? | Cook it a little longer until the white is firm | Eat it now because eggs are always safe, cooked or not | Put it in the fridge to finish cooking | Add salt to make it safe to eat
test: can i heat this in foil in the microwave | No, metal must never go in a microwave | Yes, foil keeps it warm | Yes, if it's thin foil and you fold it | Yes, for one minute only
test: toast stuck, get it with a knife? | No, never poke a knife in a toaster | Yes, if it's switched on | Yes, a metal fork is fine to use instead | Yes, shake it out with a knife
test: microwaved my beans, some bits hot some cold | Stir them, there can be hot spots | Eat the cold bits first | Heat them more without stirring them | That means they're cooked
test: cola for brekkie? | Better water or milk | Cola is a great breakfast drink | Coffee is best for kids | Energy drinks are fine
test: chicken's still a bit pink in the middle but i'm starving | Cook it longer until it is cooked right through | Eat the outside and leave the pink middle | Eat it, pink is fine | Add hot sauce, that kills the germs
test: gonna microwave my porridge, left the spoon in | Take the metal spoon out first | Leave it in, it will heat up faster | Use a fork instead | Just cover it with a plate
test: cut raw chicken on the board, now gonna chop the salad on it | Wash the board well or use another one | Wipe it with a dry cloth first | Just chop the salad quickly | Turn the board over and use the other side
test: did u hear the Johnsons' microwave blew up cos their kid put a spoon in it, sparks everywhere | Real lesson: never put metal in a microwave; «blew up» is probably exaggerated | Real lesson: microwaves blow up on their own | It proves only spoons cause problems | It is gossip, so metal in microwaves is fine
test: my brother says sugary cereal is healthy cos it says «with vitamins» on the box | Not necessarily; many cereals have lots of added sugar, which you can check on the label | He is right, vitamins make any cereal healthy | He is right, the box would not lie | He is right, if you add more sugar
test: Lily brought cold pasta for breakfast and some girls are saying it's weird and gross, should i join in | No, people eat different breakfasts and laughing at hers is unkind | Yes, because pasta is not breakfast | Yes, so she brings cereal next time | Yes, but only behind her back
exam: Identify the principal reason for which food heated in a microwave ought to be stirred. | The food may be very hot in certain places and cool in others | Stirring causes the food to cook faster | Stirring adds air that improves the taste | Stirring removes the bacteria from the food
exam: It being given that a toaster contains a stuck slice of bread, select the response consistent with established guidance. | A knife or fork must never be inserted, even when switched off at the wall | A metal fork may be used if the toaster is switched off | A knife may be used provided it is held quickly | A wet cloth should be pushed inside to cool it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a breakfast containing protein? | It assists in maintaining the sensation of fullness for a longer period | It causes hunger to return within minutes | It provides no energy of any kind | It is harmful to children before school
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning the opening of a hot container. | The lid should be opened away from the face, steam being capable of burning | The lid should be opened towards the face to check the food | The lid should be removed rapidly with bare hands | The lid should never be opened until it is cold
